1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,130
On Saturday, the eleventh of June, 2016,

2
00:00:03,197 --> 00:00:07,890
a woman in a red gown walked
onto the stage at Wembley Arena.

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:10,760
Thousands of her investors
filled the arena.

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:15,450
She promised them that in two years,
nobody would speak about Bitcoin anymore.

5
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:19,020
She was selling investors
the coin that would kill it.

6
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:21,250
That coin did not exist.

7
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:23,548
This is the story of how a product

8
00:00:23,615 --> 00:00:26,860
that never existed raised more
than four billion dollars.

9
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:35,500
No blockchain. No mining. No market. Just
a database her own company controlled.

10
00:00:35,930 --> 00:00:38,862
But that's not the whole story.
Two years before Wembley,

11
00:00:38,929 --> 00:00:40,770
she had already written the ending.

12
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:45,780
An email from August 2014,
listing her exit strategy.

13
00:00:46,210 --> 00:00:49,530
RUJA: Option one: "Take the money and
run and blame someone else for this."

14
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:53,680
She did all three. Almost to the word.

15
00:00:54,110 --> 00:00:57,765
So who paid for it? Three
and a half million investors,

16
00:00:57,832 --> 00:00:59,930
and the money is still missing.

17
00:01:00,353 --> 00:01:06,493
Ruja Ignatova was born in Ruse, Bulgaria,
in 1980, and grew up in Germany.

18
00:01:06,923 --> 00:01:10,396
On paper, the founder was the
most credible person in any room.

19
00:01:10,463 --> 00:01:13,933
A real law doctorate from
the University of Konstanz.

20
00:01:14,363 --> 00:01:15,893
A job at McKinsey.

21
00:01:16,563 --> 00:01:21,093
Her marketing added Oxford. Her
Oxford degree was a master's.

22
00:01:21,523 --> 00:01:27,093
What the marketing left out: in 2012,
a German court convicted her of fraud.

23
00:01:27,523 --> 00:01:32,360
She and her father had stripped a foundry
until it went bankrupt. Her sentence:

24
00:01:32,427 --> 00:01:34,103
fourteen months, suspended.

25
00:01:34,533 --> 00:01:38,200
Then, in 2013, she tried a
first crypto scheme, BigCoin,

26
00:01:38,267 --> 00:01:41,933
alongside a Swedish salesman
named Sebastian Greenwood.

27
00:01:42,363 --> 00:01:46,563
In June 2014 she sent Greenwood
an email pitching a bigger deal.

28
00:01:46,983 --> 00:01:48,046
In her words:

29
00:01:48,113 --> 00:01:49,693
RUJA: "you as the magic sales machine,

30
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:52,013
and me as someone who really
can work with numbers…"

31
00:01:52,443 --> 00:01:54,563
RUJA: "…like MLM meets
bitch of wall street."

32
00:01:54,993 --> 00:01:59,139
In their private emails, the two founders
even had a nickname for their product:

33
00:01:59,206 --> 00:01:59,943
"trashy coin."

34
00:02:00,373 --> 00:02:02,613
Two months later came the exit email.

35
00:02:03,033 --> 00:02:07,843
So before a single coin was sold, the
plan had three steps: take, run, blame.

36
00:02:08,253 --> 00:02:11,813
Step one. Here's the trick:
investors never actually bought a coin.

37
00:02:12,243 --> 00:02:14,038
They bought an education package:

38
00:02:14,105 --> 00:02:18,673
course notes that a US lawsuit says were
copied from free sources like Wikipedia.

39
00:02:19,103 --> 00:02:23,873
Package prices started at one
hundred euros and went up to 118,000.

40
00:02:24,303 --> 00:02:28,163
Each package came with tokens,
and you "mined" the tokens into coins.

41
00:02:28,593 --> 00:02:32,853
Mining meant a timer. When it ran out,
a number appeared in your account.

42
00:02:33,283 --> 00:02:36,996
And the price of that number?
The office in Sofia typed it in.

43
00:02:37,063 --> 00:02:42,603
Fifty euro cents at launch.
Twenty-nine ninety-five by 2019.

44
00:02:43,033 --> 00:02:46,303
Yet the coin never traded
on a single public market.

45
00:02:46,733 --> 00:02:49,693
Then came the splits: every few months,
your tokens doubled overnight.

46
00:02:50,133 --> 00:02:54,646
But each time, the "mining
difficulty" quietly rose:

47
00:02:54,713 --> 00:02:57,443
from 44 tokens per coin to 359.

48
00:02:57,863 --> 00:03:00,383
So on screen, your balance only ever grew.

49
00:03:00,813 --> 00:03:03,236
The company promised 120 billion coins.

50
00:03:03,303 --> 00:03:06,303
Bitcoin will only ever
have twenty-one million.

51
00:03:06,733 --> 00:03:08,463
And every buyer was also a salesman.

52
00:03:08,933 --> 00:03:11,933
You got paid on your recruits,
and on your recruits' recruits.

53
00:03:12,373 --> 00:03:16,096
At the very top, Greenwood took
five percent of all sales worldwide:

54
00:03:16,163 --> 00:03:18,113
over three hundred million dollars.

55
00:03:18,543 --> 00:03:25,243
But your own bonus? Sixty percent cash.
Forty percent locked inside the system.

56
00:03:25,673 --> 00:03:27,743
And even the cash was hard to get out.

57
00:03:28,173 --> 00:03:32,056
The only exchange was OneCoin's
own, with daily selling limits,

58
00:03:32,123 --> 00:03:33,753
and it kept going offline.

59
00:03:34,183 --> 00:03:36,403
Which meant the money flowed one way: in.

60
00:03:36,823 --> 00:03:44,903
OneCoin's own files, later obtained by US
prosecutors, cover late 2014 to late 2016.

61
00:03:45,333 --> 00:03:48,383
Sales: four point zero
three seven billion euros.

62
00:03:48,813 --> 00:03:51,603
Booked as profit: two point
seven three five billion.

63
00:03:52,033 --> 00:03:56,083
That's two of every three euros, because
the product cost almost nothing to make.

64
00:03:56,513 --> 00:03:58,345
For scale: at the end of 2016,

65
00:03:58,412 --> 00:04:03,473
all the Bitcoin in the world was worth
about fifteen and a half billion dollars.

66
00:04:03,893 --> 00:04:08,693
OneCoin had already raised more than a
quarter of that. For rows in a database.

67
00:04:09,123 --> 00:04:13,183
In June 2016, at the height of the
money, she took the stage at Wembley.

68
00:04:13,613 --> 00:04:17,954
And that September, Greenwood sent her
brother a message about the members:

69
00:04:18,021 --> 00:04:19,373
"These ppl are idiots."

70
00:04:19,813 --> 00:04:23,853
Her brother's message back: "the network
would not work with intelligent people."

71
00:04:24,287 --> 00:04:29,037
But a fake blockchain has one weakness:
one day, you need a real programmer.

72
00:04:29,467 --> 00:04:33,253
In October 2016, OneCoin
launched a brand new blockchain,

73
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:35,687
and doubled every member's balance.

74
00:04:36,117 --> 00:04:37,052
That same season,

75
00:04:37,119 --> 00:04:41,367
a Norwegian developer named Bjørn
Bjercke took a recruiter's phone call.

76
00:04:41,797 --> 00:04:45,257
The job: build a blockchain
for a cryptocurrency company.

77
00:04:45,687 --> 00:04:49,086
"Well, first of all, they need a
blockchain," the recruiter said.

78
00:04:49,153 --> 00:04:51,127
"They don't have a blockchain today."

79
00:04:51,557 --> 00:04:54,977
Bjercke said no. Then he started talking.

80
00:04:55,407 --> 00:05:00,127
By then, regulators in Norway, Germany
and Britain had already moved against it.

81
00:05:00,557 --> 00:05:06,267
In January 2017, OneCoin's internal
exchange shut down for good.

82
00:05:06,697 --> 00:05:08,647
And still, the selling didn't stop.

83
00:05:09,077 --> 00:05:12,205
Because the sales team was
the victims themselves,

84
00:05:12,272 --> 00:05:15,467
and doubting meant calling
your own family a fool.

85
00:05:15,887 --> 00:05:19,497
So who could stop a scheme that
its own investors protected?

86
00:05:19,927 --> 00:05:24,787
It wasn't a regulator.
It was her boyfriend.

87
00:05:25,217 --> 00:05:27,967
Gilbert Armenta moved money for OneCoin.

88
00:05:28,397 --> 00:05:33,277
But from 2017, he was secretly
cooperating with the FBI.

89
00:05:33,707 --> 00:05:36,659
Prosecutors later called
his help "extraordinary":

90
00:05:36,726 --> 00:05:39,807
it led to charges against
at least fourteen people.

91
00:05:40,227 --> 00:05:43,697
She was afraid someone close
to her would give her up.

92
00:05:44,127 --> 00:05:45,217
She was right.

93
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:51,237
Step two. On Saturday,
the seventh of October, 2017,

94
00:05:51,304 --> 00:05:54,850
OneCoin opened a big event in Lisbon.

95
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:57,040
Its founder never showed.

96
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:01,600
Five days later, the United States
issued a warrant for her arrest.

97
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:04,860
She told her brother she was very tired.

98
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:07,533
She had what he called a "big passport",

99
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:10,500
and he bought her tickets
to Vienna and to Athens.

100
00:06:10,930 --> 00:06:14,233
On Wednesday,
the twenty-fifth of October, 2017,

101
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:17,740
she boarded a Ryanair
plane from Sofia to Athens.

102
00:06:18,170 --> 00:06:21,840
One bag. One bodyguard.

103
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:24,013
She landed in Athens.

104
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:28,760
And that is the last confirmed
sighting of Ruja Ignatova.

105
00:06:29,180 --> 00:06:31,870
She never contacted her brother again.

106
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:34,090
But the company didn't even pause.

107
00:06:34,520 --> 00:06:38,420
Her brother, a former forklift driver,
ran it for sixteen more months.

108
00:06:38,850 --> 00:06:41,770
Members kept buying coins into 2019.

109
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:45,560
Take the money: done. Run: done.

110
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,300
One step left: blame someone else.

111
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:50,320
The blame arrived fast.

112
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:56,480
In July 2018, Greenwood was arrested
on the Thai island of Koh Samui.

113
00:06:56,910 --> 00:07:01,040
In March 2019, her brother's
plane landed in Los Angeles,

114
00:07:01,107 --> 00:07:03,390
and he was arrested on arrival.

115
00:07:03,810 --> 00:07:08,088
New York's top federal prosecutor
called OneCoin "a pyramid scheme

116
00:07:08,155 --> 00:07:11,710
based on smoke and mirrors
more than zeroes and ones."

117
00:07:12,150 --> 00:07:17,150
Then came the man who washed the money:
Mark Scott, a partner at a major law firm.

118
00:07:17,580 --> 00:07:21,720
His fake investment funds moved
about four hundred million dollars.

119
00:07:22,150 --> 00:07:24,050
His fee: more than fifty million.

120
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:26,890
His defence: he didn't know it was a scam.

121
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:29,500
The jury took about four hours.

122
00:07:29,930 --> 00:07:31,910
So line up the sentences.

123
00:07:32,340 --> 00:07:35,740
Greenwood, the salesman: twenty years.

124
00:07:36,170 --> 00:07:38,260
Scott, the lawyer: ten years.

125
00:07:38,690 --> 00:07:43,090
Armenta, the boyfriend who flipped,
then broke his own deal: five years.

126
00:07:43,520 --> 00:07:47,750
Irina Dilkinska, head of legal
and compliance: four years.

127
00:07:48,180 --> 00:07:52,870
Konstantin, who testified about his
sister's escape: time served, and free.

128
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:59,770
And Ruja? Charged on five counts, never
tried. In law, she is presumed innocent.

129
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:02,330
Then even her escape got a sequel.

130
00:08:02,770 --> 00:08:08,421
Frank Schneider, a former Luxembourg
spy and OneCoin's security adviser,

131
00:08:08,488 --> 00:08:10,950
was arrested in France in 2021.

132
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:17,870
Under house arrest, with a GPS tag on
his ankle, he vanished in May 2023.

133
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:20,130
He is still a fugitive.

134
00:08:20,553 --> 00:08:21,913
So where is she?

135
00:08:22,753 --> 00:08:27,117
In 2022, the FBI put her on
its Ten Most Wanted list,

136
00:08:27,184 --> 00:08:29,943
the only woman on it at the time.

137
00:08:30,373 --> 00:08:35,843
In 2024, the United States raised
the reward to five million dollars.

138
00:08:36,273 --> 00:08:41,363
A leaked Bulgarian police memo
claims she was killed in 2018.

139
00:08:41,783 --> 00:08:47,363
The document is real. But the claim
inside it is second-hand, and unverified.

140
00:08:47,793 --> 00:08:50,813
So the FBI still hunts
her as if she's alive.

141
00:08:51,233 --> 00:08:53,983
Nobody has confirmed
seeing her since Athens.

142
00:08:54,413 --> 00:08:56,313
What we can confirm is the bill.

143
00:08:56,743 --> 00:09:02,533
In Uganda, the BBC found farmers who
had sold land and livestock to buy in.

144
00:09:02,963 --> 00:09:03,837
In Germany,

145
00:09:03,904 --> 00:09:07,258
investigators traced the money of 17,552

146
00:09:07,325 --> 00:09:11,003
victims through a single
payment processor.

147
00:09:11,433 --> 00:09:15,023
In Glasgow, Jen McAdam had
brought in friends and family.

148
00:09:15,453 --> 00:09:20,043
Together, she said, they had "thrown
a quarter of a million euros away."

149
00:09:20,473 --> 00:09:26,983
In 2026, the US opened a fund for victims:
just over forty million dollars.

150
00:09:27,403 --> 00:09:32,643
Against more than four billion lost,
that's about one cent on the dollar.

151
00:09:33,083 --> 00:09:37,587
Here's the real reason it worked:
the fraud never needed smarter victims.

152
00:09:37,654 --> 00:09:40,283
It needed one person to open the database.

153
00:09:40,713 --> 00:09:45,063
Nobody could, because the "blockchain"
was private, the salesmen were family,

154
00:09:45,130 --> 00:09:46,793
and the money was everywhere.

155
00:09:47,223 --> 00:09:55,113
Take the money. Run. Blame someone
else. She wrote it in 2014.

156
00:09:55,543 --> 00:10:00,673
The coin never existed.
The exit strategy did.

